<article class="env-lemma" id="091C">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/091C">Lemma <span data-tag="091C">15.88.16</span>.</a> Let $(A_ n)$ be an inverse system of abelian groups. The following are equivalent </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$(A_ n)$ is zero as a pro-object, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$\mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits A_ n = 0$ and $R^1\mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits A_ n = 0$ and the same holds for $\bigoplus _{i \in \mathbf{N}} (A_ n)$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      It follows from Lemma <a href="/tag/0H9K" data-tag="0H9K">15.88.4</a> that (1) implies (2). Assume (2). Then $(A_ n)$ is ML by Lemma <a href="/tag/0CQA" data-tag="0CQA">15.88.14</a>. For $m \geq n$ let $A_{n, m} = \mathop{\mathrm{Im}}(A_ m \to A_ n)$ so that $A_ n = A_{n, n} \supset A_{n, n + 1} \supset \ldots $. Note that $(A_ n)$ is zero as a pro-object if and only if for every $n$ there is an $m \geq n$ such that $A_{n, m} = 0$. Note that $(A_ n)$ is ML if and only if for every $n$ there is an $m_ n \geq n$ such that $A_{n, m} = A_{n, m + 1} = \ldots $. In the ML case it is clear that $\mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits A_ n = 0$ implies that $A_{n, m_ n} = 0$ because the maps $A_{n + 1, m_{n + 1}} \to A_{n, m}$ are surjective. This finishes the proof. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>